<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Xavier de Mendonça Furtado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1701">1701</a>–<a href="/years/1769">1769</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Xavier de Mendonça Furtado was a Portuguese administrator, brother of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a>, who governed the State of Grão-Pará and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> from 1751 to 1759. He led the demarcation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> borders under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a>, curtailed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions and set up a trading company. He later became Portugal's secretary of state for the navy and colonies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-xavier-de-mendonca-furtado.jpg" alt="Francisco Xavier de Mendonça Furtado" width="320" height="525" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Francisco_Xavier_de_Mendon%C3%A7a_Furtado.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
